n: 3.14.3.6
name: what-the-statements-say
kind: finding
is: Read together, the owner's statements say six things: state belongs to a node and is not the node's kind; a move removes nothing; moves are invited by measurement, not forced; ice is shared as it is, water is serialized and re-addressed, gas is in RAM; ice is kept small; compiled code is a cached, regenerable artifact and not ice.
status: inferred
conf: mid
from: 3.14.1.2 3.14.1.3 3.14.3.1 3.14.3.2 3.14.3.3 3.14.3.4 3.14.3.5
limit: The mission sentence "the multiple states of nodes (ice, water, gas) are respected" reached the reading only as relayed by the workflow and is assumed verbatim; if the original says more, it outranks the reading's proposal.
at: 2026-11-24

n: 3.14.3.7
name: attestation-is-thin
kind: finding
is: No definition file written by the owner was found. Every long definition after 2025 is assistant text or unattested, and the 2025 text cannot be shown not to be assistant-drafted. A quotation in a commit message is an assistant's record of what the owner said; the session transcripts behind the quoted commits were not opened.
status: inferred
conf: high
from: 3.14.1.2 3.14.3.2 3.14.3.3 3.14.3.5
limit: In the old body 1,634 commits carry the owner's git name and no trailer, which cannot tell a person from an agent that writes no trailer, so "unattested" means only that no owner statement was found. The reading's summary counts seven recorded statements where its table lists nine rows for 2026-05-27 to 2026-09-08, eight without a memory note. The Living Codex has 222 commits, 221 under the owner's full name and one under a second author name.
at: 2026-11-24

n: 3.14.4
name: readings-and-conflicts
kind: hub
is: Twelve of the uses are one idea on different surfaces: ice is what others rely on by identity, water is held and worked by a holder, gas is relied on by no one. Five conflict with it: kind as state, compiled form as ice (two uses), idea lifecycle, and queue pressure. The kind binding still stands in three files or more. The oldest definition says what the states are; the reading of 2026-06-08 to 2026-06-10 says when a node moves and who can reach it. In the oldest definition compiled code is gas.
at: 2026-11-24

n: 3.14.4.1
name: one-compatible-reading
kind: finding
is: Twelve of the 21 uses fit one reading: ice is the immutable, durable thing others may rely on by identity; water is a materialized, live thing a holder is working with, lost or serialized when the holder ends; gas is a thing no one else relies on, droppable because it can be derived again or was never committed.
status: inferred
conf: mid
from: 3.14.1.1 3.14.1.3 3.14.2.4 3.14.2.5 3.14.2.7 3.14.2.8 3.14.2.9
limit: The reading's table has 22 rows (D1 to D21 and D2'); its count of 12 compatible, 5 conflicting and 4 loose leaves the plasma row apart, and one row (memory horizon) is listed both as loosely compatible and as loose metaphor.
at: 2026-11-24

n: 3.14.4.3
name: compiled-code-was-gas
kind: finding
is: On whether compiled code is ice or gas, the oldest definition, of 2025-09-09, does not mention compiled code and puts generated prototypes in water. The second, of 2025-09-12 (commit 721e2c7), answers gas for the compiled binary and water for generated source: only the spec is ice, and code and binaries are regenerable caches. Later assistant or unattested text reversed the assignment, calling hot native code crystallized and the on-disk image ice. The word moved from the small thing that must not be lost to a large cache.
status: inferred
conf: high
from: 3.14.1.2 3.14.1.3 3.14.2.6 3.14.2.7 3.14.3.1
at: 2026-11-24

n: 3.14.8
name: keep
kind: hub
is: The reading's own proposal, found in no single source and not put to the owner: the state of a node is the reach of its commitment, independent of its kind; three checkable invariants (only water is mutable, a node references only nodes at least as committed, a transition conserves the node); ice marked essential or pinned, with essential ice kept small and compiled code never essential; six named operations of which only freeze needs an offer; counts and heat are readings that invite a move.
at: 2026-11-24

n: 3.14.8.2
name: three-states-defined
kind: finding
is: Proposal: gas is committed to no one, private to one evaluation or process, and nothing is lost if it goes. Water is committed to its holder, mutable by the holder only, and lives while the holder lives. Ice is committed to everyone, identified by its canonical bytes, never mutated, durable and shared by identity.
status: inferred
conf: mid
from: 3.14.4.1 3.14.3.4 3.14.3.5 3.14.2.8
limit: One earlier use gives gas little or no body (an identity barely instantiated), another a full node population in private RAM; the proposal allows both and leaves open whether another node may address a gas node. Water folds a read-only cache and a mutable draft into one state.
at: 2026-11-24

n: 3.14.8.3
name: only-water-is-mutable
kind: finding
is: Proposed invariant 1: among nodes with a reader other than their maker, only water is mutable, and only by its holder. Ice is never written after birth. Gas has no reader but its maker and may change freely.
status: inferred
conf: mid
from: 3.14.8.2
at: 2026-11-24

n: 3.14.8.4
name: reference-only-as-committed
kind: finding
is: Proposed invariant 2: a node references only nodes at least as committed as itself. Ice points only at ice, so its identity is computable and means the same everywhere; water points at water and ice; gas at anything. The Living Codex edge rule and the frozen file of the 2026-11-05 carrier already follow this rule.
status: inferred
conf: mid
from: 3.14.1.4 3.14.2.9 1.3
at: 2026-11-24

n: 3.14.8.5
name: transition-conserves-the-node
kind: finding
is: Proposed invariant 3: a transition conserves the node and never changes its kind. A transition changes where the body is held and who may rely on it. With identity computed from content, the identity is conserved only while the content is unchanged.
status: inferred
conf: mid
from: 3.14.3.2 3.14.3.3 1.3
at: 2026-11-24

n: 3.14.8.7
name: six-named-operations
kind: finding
is: Proposed operations: condense (gas to water) materializes a body; freeze (water to ice) computes the identity and checks every child is ice; melt (ice to water) opens a mutable copy and leaves the ice as it is; evaporate (water to gas) releases the body; sublimate (ice to gas) drops the bytes and keeps the identity; thaw reads ice and verifies it.
status: inferred
conf: mid
from: 3.14.1.4 3.14.2.3 3.14.2.4 3.14.2.7 3.14.2.9 3.14.3.2
limit: The name evaporate is taken from the 2026-02 specs, where it meant loading into the runtime. A direct move from gas to ice is condense followed by freeze and gets no name.
at: 2026-11-24

n: 3.14.8.8
name: freeze-is-offered-readings-invite
kind: finding
is: Proposal: freeze is the one transition that needs an offer, because it creates something others will rely on; the holder offers and the store acknowledges. Sublimate is refused for essential ice that is referenced. Counts and heat are readings that invite a freeze, a melt, a sublimate or a pin; a reading performs nothing.
status: inferred
conf: mid
from: 1.5 3.14.1.2 3.14.2.4 3.14.3.2
limit: Which of the four acknowledgments a freeze returns was not examined. Whether unreferenced essential ice may lose its bytes without consent is not settled.
at: 2026-11-24

